// A 'So' owner held in a local, handed back where a borrow is declared: the
// local is ended at the return, so the borrow would point at freed memory --
// unless the value is never freed, which is a leak.
//
// Verdict: refuse, ErrorEscape at the returned owner.
// Basis: refborref.html, "Lifetime Constraints" (a borrow of a local may not
// outlive its block) -- the coercion to '&R' is a borrow of what the local
// owns; and, by reading of the rules, a borrow through an owner is bounded by
// whoever holds the owner. Rust refuses 'let b = Box::new(r); &*b' returned
// (E0515, "returns a value referencing data owned by the current function";
// by reading, not run).
// Master d331baab: accepted, and it leaks -- main, run, prints "read = 42"
// and "end" but never "final": the owner is never ended. Stage 1 fixed it.

mod lifetime_band_return_owner_local_as_borrow;

import stdio use *;

struct R {
  pub n i64;
  fn final(self &uni) {
    printStr("final\n");
  }
}

fn make() &R {
  imm s = new So[R](42);
  s;                         //~ ErrorEscape
}

fn main() {
  {
    imm r = make();
    printStr("read = ");
    printInt(r.n);
    printStr("\n");
  }
  printStr("end\n");
}
